# .gitattributes — iHymns
#
# Exact copies of the shared language policy (MWBM-MEDIA-LANG) and its files.
#
# These files are checked byte for byte against the master copies in
# MWBMPartners/MeedyaSuite-core by tools/media-lang/check_copies.py, which runs
# in CI. A copy whose bytes git has changed no longer matches the master's
# checksum, and the check then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with
# its content. Never edit these files here: change the master and take the new
# version with the checker's --update mode.
#
# What the four settings on each line below do: they stop git itself from
# changing a copy's bytes when it writes or reads the file —
#   -text                    no line-ending conversion (for example on a
#                            Windows checkout with core.autocrlf);
#   -filter                  no clean/smudge filter;
#   -working-tree-encoding   no conversion to another character encoding;
#   -ident                   no expansion of a "$Id$" marker into a checksum.
# They win over any EARLIER line in this file that also matches a copy, and
# over the global and system attributes files (a person's own
# core.attributesFile, and git's system-wide file), which git always gives the
# LOWEST priority.
#
# What they CANNOT stop — each of these wins over the lines below:
#   - any LATER line in this same file that also matches a copy: git uses the
#     last matching line for each setting, so that line does not have to be
#     broader ("* text=auto", "*.md filter=…", or the copy's own path again);
#   - a .gitattributes file closer to a copy: in the copy's own folder, or in a
#     folder between the copy and the top of the repository (a deeper file
#     wins over a shallower one). A .gitattributes in some other sub-folder,
#     one that does not contain the copy, has no effect on it;
#   - .git/info/attributes on someone's own machine, because git always gives
#     that file the HIGHEST priority.
# (Checked with "git check-attr" on git 2.55, #2137 review rounds 7 and 8, and
# against git's own gitattributes documentation.) If any of these changes a
# copy's bytes, the copy checker reports the copy as changed: that is the
# checker doing its job, not a fault in the copy.
docs/standards/media-language-bcp47-policy.md -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
docs/standards/MWBM-MEDIA-LANG.lock -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
docs/standards/data/bcp47-language-data-v1.schema.json -text -filter -working-tree-encoding -ident
